Then add three helper columns in Excel
Column	Formula (row 2)
maxSizeGB	=IF(J2<=0,"",J2/1073741824)
capacityUnit	=IF(OR(E2="Standard",E2="Basic",E2="Premium"),"DTU","vCore")
matchKey	=B2&" / "&C2

The first one is important — it turns Hyperscale's -1 into a blank instead of that -9.31E-10. A blank correctly means "not applicable."

capacityUnit is what stops anyone reading 800 as 800 processor cores.

Sheet 3 — Storage used & allocated ❌

You were right: Resource Graph can't give you this. It only holds the ceiling you provisioned. Actual consumption lives in Azure Monitor metrics, which means one call per database.

Run this in Cloud Shell (PowerShell):

$rows = az graph query -q "resources
  | where type=='microsoft.sql/servers/databases' and name != 'master'
  | project id, name, server=tostring(split(id,'/')[8])" `
  --first 1000 -o json | ConvertFrom-Json

$start = (Get-Date).AddDays(-7).ToString("yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ")
$out = @()
$i = 0

foreach ($db in $rows.data) {
  $i++
  Write-Host "$i/$($rows.data.Count)  $($db.server)/$($db.name)"
  try {
    $m = az monitor metrics list --resource $db.id `
      --metric "storage" "allocated_data_storage" `
      --interval PT6H --aggregation Maximum `
      --start-time $start -o json 2>$null | ConvertFrom-Json

    $used  = ($m.value | Where-Object { $_.name.value -eq 'storage' }
              ).timeseries.data.maximum | Measure-Object -Maximum |
              Select-Object -Expand Maximum
    $alloc = ($m.value | Where-Object { $_.name.value -eq 'allocated_data_storage' }
              ).timeseries.data.maximum | Measure-Object -Maximum |
              Select-Object -Expand Maximum
  } catch { $used = $null; $alloc = $null }

  $out += [pscustomobject]@{
    server      = $db.server
    database    = $db.name
    matchKey    = "$($db.server) / $($db.name)"
    usedGB      = if ($used)  { [math]::Round($used/1GB,2) }  else { $null }
    allocatedGB = if ($alloc) { [math]::Round($alloc/1GB,2) } else { $null }
  }
}

$out | Export-Csv storage-usage.csv -NoTypeInformation
Write-Host "Done - download storage-usage.csv from Manage files"


Then Manage files → Download → storage-usage.csv → paste into a new Sheet3.

⚠️ Expect blanks, and know what they mean
The six Inaccessible databases will return nothing. A locked database emits no metrics. Blank here does not mean zero storage.
Long-paused databases may have no recent data points. That's why I set the window to 7 days.
Everything else should populate.
The three numbers and why all three matter
	Meaning
maxSizeGB (Sheet2)	The ceiling — what General Purpose bills you for
allocatedGB	Disk the files have claimed. Your floor — you can't set max below this
usedGB	Data actually inside the files

Always used ≤ allocated ≤ max.

So the saving you can take today, with no data-file work, is max − allocated. The gap between allocated and used needs a file shrink first, which is a separate and slower job.

Sheet 4 — Utilisation ❌ Do this later

Don't run this yet. Pull metrics only for the shortlist you actually intend to change — maybe 10–15 databases. Fetching CPU history for 97 databases you'll never touch wastes an hour.

When you get there, the metrics differ by model:

Model	Metrics
vCore / Serverless	cpu_percent, app_cpu_billed, app_cpu_percent, app_memory_percent
DTU (Standard/Basic)	dtu_consumption_percent
Both	connection_successful

Pull 30 days, and take both Average and Maximum. Average alone hides spikes; maximum alone hides how idle something is. You need both to defend a tier change.

Sheet 5 — Analysis

Build this only once Sheets 1–3 are populated. Every row keys off matchKey.

Groups A and B — pull straight from Sheet2 with INDEX/MATCH on matchKey.

Group C — maxSizeGB from Sheet2, allocatedGB and usedGB from Sheet3, then:

Reclaimable GB = maxSizeGB − allocatedGB

Group D — the cost split, all SUMIFS against Sheet1 on matchKey:

Column	Filter
Compute	Meter contains vCore or DTU
Storage	Meter contains Data Stored
Licence	ServiceTier contains Licen
Backup	Meter contains Backup
Defender	ServiceName contains Defender

This is the fix for the problem you spotted — one lumped total hides whether a database's cost is compute (right-size it) or storage (shrink or delete it).

Group E — leave blank until Sheet4 exists.

Group F — flags, all calculated from B/C/D.

Group G — manual. And do not skip the first two: Reservation covered? and IaC managed? A reservation makes your saving vanish; IaC silently reverts your change on the next pipeline run.

